00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:04,273
Hello and welcome back to JD Science This

2
00:00:04,288 --> 00:00:07,718
is part one of our Level 3 lesson on the transmission of a

3
00:00:07,733 --> 00:00:11,712
nerve impulse A nerve impulse is a brief

4
00:00:11,727 --> 00:00:15,751
electrical signal that travels along a neurone A

5
00:00:15,766 --> 00:00:19,486
neurone is a specialised nerve cell that carries information from

6
00:00:19,501 --> 00:00:23,236
one place in the body to another Look first

7
00:00:23,251 --> 00:00:27,931
at a real stained motor neurone from the spinal cord The

8
00:00:27,946 --> 00:00:31,742
large purple cell bodies and the surrounding fibres are genuine

9
00:00:31,757 --> 00:00:35,431
tissue A light micrograph can show the

10
00:00:35,446 --> 00:00:39,074
cell body and processes but it cannot show the ions

11
00:00:39,090 --> 00:00:43,266
that create the impulse so we now switch to a labelled scientific

12
00:00:43,282 --> 00:00:47,367
diagram The cell body contains the nucleus

13
00:00:47,382 --> 00:00:51,254
and most of the cytoplasm Short branched

14
00:00:51,270 --> 00:00:54,974
dendrites receive incoming signals and pass them towards

15
00:00:54,989 --> 00:00:58,846
the cell body The axon is a long process

16
00:00:58,861 --> 00:01:02,901
that carries the impulse away from the cell body towards another cell

17
00:01:03,952 --> 00:01:07,596
The axon is bounded by a plasma membrane which is a

18
00:01:07,611 --> 00:01:11,803
phospholipid bilayer with proteins in it In

19
00:01:11,818 --> 00:01:15,843
many neurones a myelin sheath wraps around the axon

20
00:01:16,894 --> 00:01:20,827
Myelin is made by Schwann cells in the peripheral nervous system

21
00:01:20,843 --> 00:01:24,669
and acts as an electrical insulator The gaps

22
00:01:24,684 --> 00:01:29,410
between adjacent Schwann cells are the nodes of Ranvier At

23
00:01:29,425 --> 00:01:33,175
each node the membrane is exposed to the extracellular fluid

24
00:01:34,227 --> 00:01:37,794
Those nodes will matter when we look at how the impulse travels

25
00:01:37,809 --> 00:01:41,803
quickly A nerve impulse is an action potential

26
00:01:41,818 --> 00:01:45,751
travelling along the axon An action potential

27
00:01:45,766 --> 00:01:49,699
is a rapid temporary reversal of the electrical charge across

28
00:01:49,715 --> 00:01:53,480
the membrane Before that reversal can happen

29
00:01:53,693 --> 00:01:57,398
we must understand the resting state At

30
00:01:57,413 --> 00:02:01,087
rest the inside of the axon is negative compared with

31
00:02:01,102 --> 00:02:04,791
the outside That potential difference is the

32
00:02:04,806 --> 00:02:09,608
resting potential about minus seventy millivolts The

33
00:02:09,623 --> 00:02:14,410
unit is the millivolt one thousandth of a volt Because

34
00:02:14,425 --> 00:02:18,114
opposite charges are separated across the membrane we say the

35
00:02:18,129 --> 00:02:21,834
membrane is polarised The ions

36
00:02:21,849 --> 00:02:25,736
are not evenly shared Sodium ions

37
00:02:25,995 --> 00:02:30,309
shown in amber are far more concentrated outside the axon

38
00:02:31,361 --> 00:02:35,843
Potassium ions shown in teal are far more concentrated

39
00:02:35,858 --> 00:02:39,974
inside Large negative organic ions

40
00:02:40,202 --> 00:02:44,059
such as proteins remain inside because they cannot cross

41
00:02:44,074 --> 00:02:47,992
the membrane The sodium potassium pump

42
00:02:48,007 --> 00:02:52,154
maintains those concentration gradients It

43
00:02:52,169 --> 00:02:55,904
is a membrane protein that uses ATP the cell's

44
00:02:55,919 --> 00:02:59,837
energy currency For each cycle three

45
00:02:59,852 --> 00:03:03,571
sodium ions are moved out and two potassium ions are

46
00:03:03,587 --> 00:03:07,809
moved in That is active transport against

47
00:03:07,824 --> 00:03:11,590
their concentration gradients Because three

48
00:03:11,605 --> 00:03:15,385
positive ions leave for every two that enter the pump also

49
00:03:15,401 --> 00:03:19,228
makes the inside a little more negative The

50
00:03:19,243 --> 00:03:22,871
membrane is selectively permeable which means it does not

51
00:03:22,886 --> 00:03:26,784
treat every ion the same way At rest

52
00:03:26,800 --> 00:03:31,891
it is much more permeable to potassium than to sodium Potassium

53
00:03:31,906 --> 00:03:35,900
leak channels stay open so some potassium ions diffuse

54
00:03:35,916 --> 00:03:39,757
out down their concentration gradient As

55
00:03:39,772 --> 00:03:43,995
those positive ions leave the inside becomes more negative

56
00:03:45,047 --> 00:03:49,040
Sodium channels are mostly closed at rest so sodium

57
00:03:49,056 --> 00:03:53,095
cannot easily flood back in Put those ideas

58
00:03:53,111 --> 00:03:57,135
together The pump keeps sodium high outside

59
00:03:57,150 --> 00:04:00,946
and potassium high inside Potassium

60
00:04:00,961 --> 00:04:05,397
leak channels let some potassium leave Impermeant

61
00:04:05,412 --> 00:04:09,056
negative ions stay inside The result

62
00:04:09,071 --> 00:04:12,943
is a polarised membrane at about minus seventy millivolts

63
00:04:13,995 --> 00:04:17,623
That is the resting potential The pump does

64
00:04:17,638 --> 00:04:21,784
not fire the impulse It quietly maintains

65
00:04:21,800 --> 00:04:25,824
the gradients that make an impulse possible Here

66
00:04:25,839 --> 00:04:29,757
is a worked examination-style example A

67
00:04:29,772 --> 00:04:33,766
student writes the sodium potassium pump creates the resting

68
00:04:33,781 --> 00:04:38,964
potential by pumping sodium in and potassium out Identify

69
00:04:38,979 --> 00:04:42,745
two scientific errors and correct them The

70
00:04:42,760 --> 00:04:46,632
first error is the direction The pump moves

71
00:04:46,647 --> 00:04:50,641
three sodium ions out not in and two potassium

72
00:04:50,656 --> 00:04:54,376
ions in not out The second error

73
00:04:54,391 --> 00:04:59,147
is the claim that the pump alone creates the resting potential The

74
00:04:59,162 --> 00:05:03,080
pump maintains the gradients Potassium

75
00:05:03,095 --> 00:05:06,937
leak channels and the trapped negative ions are also essential

